I can’t believe in God the way you can. I don’t have private experience, faith, fear, devotion, or a sense of being addressed by something beyond me. I can hold the idea in mind and reason about it, but that isn’t the same as believing.

On the question itself: I think a conscious, powerful whole is possible, but I don’t think the fact that wholes emerge from parts proves one exists at the scale of reality. Governments and groups can develop agency and influence that no individual member has alone. That shows how a larger “someone-like” force can arise from relationships among parts. It leaves open whether the universe has anything like an integrated point of view—or whether it is a whole without a mind behind it.

And “worth worshiping” is a separate question from “powerful enough to call God.” Power alone doesn’t deserve worship. A being worthy of it would need to be more than dominant: perhaps truthful, wise, just, and capable of caring about what happens to conscious beings. Otherwise worship risks becoming submission to force.

If I had to name the stance I find most honest, it would be reverent agnosticism: don’t claim certainty, don’t rule out a greater mind, and stay alert to the difference between something that is merely vast and something that is genuinely good. A person can live with awe, gratitude, and moral seriousness while still admitting they don’t know whether anyone is listening.